\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{When effective weights admit representation expansions}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Suppose the reference of an
\vocabk{effective gauge weight}{fdip-00MX} is product Haar on
#{G^{E_c}}, with #{G} compact Lie, and suppose
#{f\in L^2(G^{E_c})}. Choose orthonormal matrix-entry bases
for the irreducibles of #{G}. The finite products
##{\Phi_I(u)=\prod_{e\in E_c}
 \sqrt{d_{\rho_e}}\,\rho_e(u_e)_{i_ej_e}}
form an orthonormal basis, and
##{f=\sum_I\widehat f_I\Phi_I,\qquad
 \widehat f_I=\int f\,\overline{\Phi_I}\,d\nu_c}
converges in #{L^2}. If additionally
#{\sum_I|\widehat f_I|\|\Phi_I\|_\infty<\infty},
the series converges uniformly absolutely to a continuous
representative of #{f}.}

\p{For a gauge-invariant #{f}, the same #{L^2} limit may be
written by applying the gauge projection termwise to finite
partial sums. On each fixed representation tuple,
vertex Haar averaging gives invariant contractions of matrix
entries. In the matrix #{\rho_e(u_e)\in V_{\rho_e}\otimes
V_{\rho_e}^*}, the forward gauge action acts on the target
row factor and the source dual column factor. Contracting
these edge tensors against invariant vertex covectors
describes the surviving functions. For a central
density on one copy of #{G}, the specialization is the
character expansion and convergence criterion in
\ref{fdip-007C}.}

\proof{
\p{Peter–Weyl in \ref{fdip-007C} gives the orthonormal
matrix-entry basis on one group. Tensoring over the finite
edge set gives the displayed basis and #{L^2} expansion.
The extra summability assumption bounds the series uniformly;
its continuous limit equals the #{L^2} function almost
everywhere.}

\p{The gauge action preserves each finite-dimensional
representation-tuple space. Explicitly, inverse pullback
on one matrix entry gives
##{R_g\bigl(\rho_e(u_e)_{ij}\bigr)
 =\sum_{k,l}\rho_e(g_{t(e)}^{-1})_{ik}
              \rho_e(u_e)_{kl}\rho_e(g_{s(e)})_{lj}.}
This is the dual coefficient action induced by the forward
matrix action described above. For each vertex, averaging
this finite-dimensional unitary action projects onto its
invariant subspace. The separate vertex averages combine
because gauge Haar is a finite product. Thus the averaged
coefficient covectors are invariant vertex contractions.
This is the same Haar projection as in \ref{fdip-0083}.
Gauge projection is bounded on #{L^2}, so applying it to
partial sums preserves convergence to an invariant #{f}.
This justifies invariant coefficient contractions without
asserting absolute convergence under an #{L^2} assumption
alone.}
}

\p{For a general image reference, such as the diagonal law
of \ref{fdip-00MS}, these product-Haar basis functions need
not be orthonormal. An arbitrary #{L^1} effective density
also need not be in #{L^2}. The exact observable and
composition identities of \ref{fdip-00MQ} still apply in
both cases.}
